2026-10-04T00:47:06.3302738Z ### [RUSTSEC-2026-0221](https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0221.html)
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3303256Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3303632Z > `event-listener` allows `!Send` tags to cross thread boundaries via `StackSlot`
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3304162Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3304393Z | Details             |                                                |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3305015Z | ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3305592Z | Status              | unsound                |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3306167Z | Package             | `event-listener`                      |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3306862Z | Version             | `5.4.1`                   |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3307801Z | URL                 | [https://github.com/smol-rs/event-listener/pull/163](https://github.com/smol-rs/event-listener/pull/163) |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3308762Z | Date                | 2026-07-13                         |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3309125Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3309495Z Affected versions of `event-listener` unconditionally implement `Send` and
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3310367Z `Sync` for `StackSlot&lt;&#39;_, T&gt;`, the stack-allocated listener type created
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3311025Z by the `listener!` macro.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3311279Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3311584Z This allows a `!Send` tag type set via `Event::with_tag` to be moved to
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3312428Z another thread and accessed via `StackSlot::wait`, causing a data race in safe
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3313077Z code.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3313594Z ### [RUSTSEC-2024-0429](https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2024-0429.html)
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3314104Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3314766Z > Unsoundness in `Iterator` and `DoubleEndedIterator` impls for `glib::VariantStrIter`
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3315355Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3315592Z | Details             |                                                |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3316374Z | ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3316943Z | Status              | unsound                |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3317486Z | Package             | `glib`                      |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3318211Z | Version             | `0.18.5`                   |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3319109Z | URL                 | [https://github.com/gtk-rs/gtk-rs-core/pull/1343](https://github.com/gtk-rs/gtk-rs-core/pull/1343) |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3320028Z | Date                | 2024-03-30                         |
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3320394Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3321435Z The `VariantStrIter::impl_get` function (called internally by implementations of the `Iterator` and `DoubleEndedIterator` traits for this type) was unsound, resulting in undefined behaviour.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3322603Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3324860Z An immutable reference `&amp;p` to a `*mut libc::c_char` pointer initialized to `NULL` was passed as an argument to a C function that that mutates the pointer behind `&amp;p` in-place (i.e. as an out-argument), which was unsound. After changes in recent versions of the Rust compiler, these unsound writes through `&amp;p` now seem to be completely disregarded when building the `glib` crate with optimizations.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3327339Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3328791Z This subsequently caused all calls of `VariantStrIter::impl_get` to violate the safety requirements of the `std::ffi::CStr::from_ptr` function - which requires its argument to be a valid pointer to a C-style string - resulting in crashes due to `NULL` pointer dereferences.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3330404Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3330884Z This was fixed by passing the out-argument pointer explitly as `&amp;mut p` instead of `&amp;p`.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3331536Z 
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3333339Z This issue has been present since this code was initially added in `glib` v0.15.0. The mismatch in mutability was likely missed (and not raised as an error by the compiler) because the C function wrapped by `VariantStrIter::impl_get` is variadic (`glib_sys::g_variant_get_child`), and the pointer in question is one of the variadic arguments.
2026-10-04T00:47:06.3335265Z 
